    "final_answer": "If I could host a dinner party for historical figures, I would aim for a blend of creativity, intellect, and wisdom to ensure the conversation sparkles. My ideal group would include:\n\n1.  **Leonardo da Vinci** \u2013 I\u2019d love to explore his boundless curiosity. He would bring a unique perspective on how art and science intertwine, likely sketching ideas on paper napkins while discussing anatomy and flight.\n2.  **William Shakespeare** \u2013 With him at the table, the conversation would be filled with profound language and insight into human nature. He\u2019d surely offer witty observations and perhaps suggest improving the evening's dialogue with a sonnet.\n3.  **Marie Curie** \u2013 Her dedication to discovery and resilience against societal barriers would ground the conversation in science and perseverance. I\u2019d have many questions about her experiences as a woman in early 20th-century academia.\n4.  **Maya Angelou** \u2013 She would bring warmth, truth, and a deep sense of humanity to the gathering. Her wisdom on resilience and identity would balance the more intellectual discussions with emotional resonance.\n\nThe conversation might start with the nature of creativity\u2014how da Vinci and Shakespeare defined it, and how Angoulo and Curie expressed it through activism and science. I\u2019d ask da Vinci how his sketches influenced modern technology, wonder with Shakespeare about how language still defines culture, and learn from Curie and Angelou about breaking through barriers in today\u2019s world. It would be the kind of gathering where history feels alive and lessons transcend time.",
